// ---------- Neighbourhoods ----------
// verdict: 'yes' (go, repeatedly) | 'once' (worth one visit) | 'through' (pass through, don't aim for) | 'skip'
// london / nyc: the closest vibe match, not a compliment. avoid: what not to do there.
const HOODS = [
 { k:'wsw', lbl:[39.9452,-75.1580], name:'Washington Square West & Midtown Village', short:'Wash West', tag:'your neighbourhood', verdict:'yes', london:'Soho, with Fitzrovia rents', nyc:'the West Village / Chelsea border',
   poly:['14/Chestnut','7/Chestnut','7/South','14/South'],
   worth:'You live here, so the question is moot, but you did well: it is the most useful address in the city. Everything on this map that is not Fishtown or West Philly is under 25 minutes on foot.',
   vibe:'Three neighbourhoods wearing one trench coat. Midtown Village (11th to Broad, Market to Locust) is the restaurant-row bit: 13th Street is Philly\'s attempt at a Soho dining strip, and about 60% of it works (Barbuzzo, Double Knot, El Vez, Sampan, Charlie was a Sinner) while 40% is Instagram bait with queues (Green Eggs, the brunch places). The Gayborhood (roughly 11th to 13th, Walnut to Pine, rainbow crosswalks) is the real, old, lived-in gay district: Woody\'s, Tavern on Camac\'s upstairs piano bar, Giovanni\'s Room bookshop, and it is the reason the neighbourhood is safe and awake at 1am. South of Locust it goes residential and pretty fast: Spruce, Pine and Delancey between 9th and 13th are brownstones, Antique Row is Pine, and Washington Square itself is a proper leafy square with almost nobody in it. Jefferson hospital and the students give the eastern half a slightly institutional feel; the northern edge (Chestnut/Market) is chains and a bit grubby.',
   avoid:['Chestnut and Market Streets between 8th and 13th after dark: not dangerous, just bleak (chains, shuttered shopfronts, the Fashion District mall).','The 13th St brunch queues on weekends. Go weekday or go to K\'Far.','Bars on 12th/13th at 2am on a Saturday if you want to sleep: you live 100 m away.'],
   known:['13th Street restaurant row','the Gayborhood and its bars','Antique Row (Pine St, 9th to 12th)','Washington Square (a mass grave of Revolutionary soldiers under the lawn, so, quiet)','Jefferson hospital and students'],
   main:['Wash Sq: read on a bench, 4 min away','McGillin\'s (1860, oldest tavern in the city, literally behind your flat)','Reading Terminal Market is a 9-min walk north','Vetri, Vedge, Talula\'s Garden for the big nights, all within 6 min','Middle Child, High Street, Merkaz for lunch'] },
 { k:'rit', lbl:[39.9522,-75.1775], name:'Rittenhouse Square', short:'Rittenhouse', tag:'the polished centre', verdict:'yes', london:'Marylebone', nyc:'the Upper West Side\'s good bits, or Gramercy',
   poly:['14/Market','24/Market','24.3/Pine','14/Pine'],
   worth:'Yes, constantly, and not for the shopping. It has the densest cluster of restaurants and bars you will actually like, the best square in the city, and the prettiest residential streets. It is also where the money is, so it can feel like Marylebone: comfortable, well-fed, a bit self-satisfied.',
   vibe:'The square (a Penn original, redesigned 1913, the model for every "urban square" argument in America) is the centre: dogs, benches, buskers, the goat statue children climb on, the Saturday farmers market. Walnut Street on the east side is chains (Apple, Anthropologie in a mansion, Sephora) and not interesting. The interesting streets are one block either side: Sansom (Her Place, Oyster House, Wine Dive, Joseph Fox Bookshop, dive bars, jazz), Chestnut (Boyds, Joan Shepp, cocktail bars), and then south of Locust the residential grid of Spruce, Delancey, Pine, Panama, Cypress: 19th-century brownstones, wisteria, gas lamps on Delancey Place between 18th and 22nd, the Rosenbach, the Curtis Institute. West of 20th it quietens further towards Fitler Square and the river. Rittenhouse is where the good cocktail bars are (Franklin Mortgage, Ranstead Room, a.bar, Andra Hem), the good breakfast (K\'Far), and about a third of the restaurants worth booking. Its weakness is soul: it can be a bit hedge-fund, and the Rittenhouse Row "scene" (Rouge on the square, Parc\'s terrace) is people watching people.',
   avoid:['Rouge, Devon, the terrace restaurants on the square itself: overpriced, mediocre, purely for being seen. Parc is the exception, and only for the terrace at 6pm.','Walnut Street as a "shopping" destination: it is a high street.','Sunday afternoon on the square in September: it is lovely and 5,000 people know it.'],
   known:['the Square (Saturday market 9-2, Tuesday too)','Sansom St: Her Place, Oyster House, Wine Dive, Joseph Fox Bookshop','Delancey Place and the Rosenbach','Curtis Institute of Music (free student recitals)','the cocktail bars: Franklin Mortgage, Ranstead Room, a.bar, Andra Hem'],
   main:['Her Place Supper Club and Friday Saturday Sunday are the two bookings to make first','Delancey Place between 18th and 22nd is the prettiest street in Center City','Open Streets: West Walnut closes Walnut to cars on Sundays through October','K\'Far breakfast, then the square'] },
 { k:'fit', lbl:[39.9450,-75.1805], name:'Fitler Square', short:'Fitler Sq', tag:'quiet, leafy, riverside', verdict:'yes', london:'the quiet streets behind Primrose Hill', nyc:'the West Village west of Hudson St',
   poly:['21/Pine','24.5/Pine','24.5/South','21/South'],
   worth:'Yes, but as a walk, not a night out. It is where the river path starts and where two or three of the best small restaurants are. Nothing happens here after 10pm, which is the point.',
   vibe:'The south-west corner of Rittenhouse, from about 21st to the Schuylkill, Spruce to South. Tiny park with a fountain and a bear statue, brick rowhouses, wisteria, a Saturday farmers market smaller than Rittenhouse\'s, and the Schuylkill Banks boardwalk two blocks west. It is the neighbourhood people mean when they say Philadelphia is "so liveable". Trattoria Carina and Sally are on the corners, Illata is the little BYOB, Rival Bros the coffee. Almost no bars. If you want a quiet, pretty, slightly smug residential walk with the river at the end, this is it.',
   avoid:['Coming here expecting nightlife: there is none.','The South Street bridge at rush hour if you are walking to Penn: fine, just loud. Walnut bridge is prettier.'],
   known:['Schuylkill Banks boardwalk and river trail','the little square and its Saturday market','Trattoria Carina, Sally, Illata'],
   main:['Start the river walk here at sunset: down the boardwalk to South St bridge, back via Grays Ferry','Rival Bros for coffee, Illata for the small BYOB night'] },
 { k:'cc', lbl:[39.9562,-75.1655], name:'City Hall, Avenue of the Arts & Market East', short:'Center City core', tag:'the spine', verdict:'once', london:'Holborn / Kingsway with a bit of Oxford Street', nyc:'Midtown around Herald Square',
   poly:['15/Vine','7/Vine','7/Chestnut','13/Chestnut','13/Lombard','15/Lombard'],
   worth:'A set of destinations, not a place to be. City Hall tower, Reading Terminal, PAFA, the orchestra: each is worth it. Hanging around between them is not.',
   vibe:'City Hall (the largest municipal building in the US, 30 years to build, William Penn on top) sits at the centre of the grid with Dilworth Park and LOVE Park either side. South of it, Broad Street becomes the "Avenue of the Arts": the Kimmel Center (Philadelphia Orchestra), the Academy of Music (1857, Broadway tours), the Wilma, the Merriam, hotels, and after 8pm on a non-show night, not much. East of City Hall along Market is the Convention Center, Reading Terminal Market (the one unmissable thing here), the Fashion District mall (a failed shopping centre, avoid), Jefferson Station, and blocks that go from fine to bleak between 8th and 11th: empty storefronts, some visible drug use around 8th and Market/Filbert. It is Philadelphia\'s Midtown: you cross it, you do the thing you came for, you leave.',
   avoid:['Market Street between 7th and 12th after dark, and the Fashion District mall at any hour.','Eating anywhere on Market or Chestnut here except Reading Terminal.','The chain restaurants around the Convention Center; you are 10 minutes from Chinatown.'],
   known:['Reading Terminal Market (12th & Arch, since 1893)','City Hall tower observation deck ($10, book online)','Kimmel Center / Marian Anderson Hall: Philadelphia Orchestra','PAFA (Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts) and its 2026 show A Nation of Artists','Masonic Temple tours','Dilworth and LOVE Park'],
   main:['Reading Terminal on a weekday morning: DiNic\'s roast pork, Beiler\'s donuts, Bassett\'s ice cream, Amish stalls Tue-Sat','City Hall tower at golden hour','An orchestra night at Marian Anderson Hall (rush tickets exist)'] },
 { k:'chi', lbl:[39.9576,-75.1548], name:'Chinatown', short:'Chinatown', tag:'small, dense, late', verdict:'yes', london:'Chinatown, but less touristy and with better food', nyc:'Manhattan Chinatown around Mott St, shrunk',
   poly:['12/Arch','8/Arch','8/Vine','12/Vine'],
   worth:'Yes, for food, repeatedly. It is eight minutes from your door and has the best cheap eating in Center City, plus Hop Sing (the city\'s best cocktail bar) and Good Good Comedy. Nothing to see; everything to eat.',
   vibe:'Ten square blocks under the Friendship Gate at 10th & Arch, hemmed in by the Convention Center and the Vine Street Expressway (which nearly killed it in the 1990s and is still fighting a proposed Sixers arena next door in 2026, a live political fight you will hear about). Cantonese roast duck in windows (Sang Kee), Shanghai soup dumplings (Dim Sum Garden), hand-pulled noodles (Nan Zhou), Burmese (Rangoon), Fujianese, Vietnamese, hot pot, bubble tea, late-night (David\'s Mai Lai Wah until 3am), a Taiwanese siphon-coffee house (Ray\'s), and TingTing\'s new HK cafe. Grimy in the way good Chinatowns are. Best on foot, best late, best hungry.',
   avoid:['The tourist-facing places right by the gate; walk two blocks in.','Trying to "see" it: there is nothing to see except the gate and the food.','9th & Vine end at night is quiet and a bit desolate; stick to Race/Arch/10th.'],
   known:['Sang Kee Peking Duck','Dim Sum Garden and Nan Zhou hand-drawn noodles on Race','Hop Sing Laundromat (rules, no phones, no photos)','Good Good Comedy Theatre','Chinatown Night Market (Oct 8)'],
   main:['Duck at Sang Kee, then Hop Sing if you get in','Ray\'s Cafe & Tea House siphon coffee is a genuine oddity','Rangoon for Burmese, nobody talks about it'] },
 { k:'old', lbl:[39.9550,-75.1435], name:'Old City', short:'Old City', tag:'history + galleries + Starr', verdict:'once', london:'the City on a Sunday, then Covent Garden on a Saturday night', nyc:'the South Street Seaport / lower Financial District',
   poly:['6.3/Walnut','1/Walnut',[39.9498,-75.1400],[39.9536,-75.1392],[39.9560,-75.1400],'6.3/Vine'],
   worth:'Once for the history (it is 2026, the 250th anniversary, so it is genuinely the centre of the country\'s attention), then back for First Fridays and two or three specific restaurants and bars. Not a neighbourhood to hang around in at weekends.',
   vibe:'Two things at once. West of 3rd Street: Independence Hall, the Liberty Bell, Carpenters\' Hall, Franklin Court, Christ Church, the Museum of the American Revolution: national-park lawns, tour groups, school parties, and in this particular year, Semiquincentennial everything. Do it early on a weekday and it is properly moving; do it on a Saturday in September and it is Disneyland. East of 3rd towards the Delaware: cobbled streets, cast-iron warehouses, Elfreth\'s Alley (1703, the oldest inhabited street in the country and two minutes long), 40-odd galleries that open late on First Fridays, Menagerie coffee, Forsythia and Fork for serious dinners, Almanac and Sassafras for drinks. And then, Friday and Saturday nights, the bridge-and-tunnel bar strip on 2nd and Market/Chestnut: sports bars, bachelorette parties, the Continental, bouncers. It is Philly\'s Covent Garden problem in miniature. The riverfront (Race Street Pier, Cherry Street Pier, Spruce Street Harbor Park until late Sept) is better than it sounds and mostly ignored.',
   avoid:['2nd Street bars on Friday/Saturday night unless you want a stag-do crowd.','Buddakan, Continental, Amada, Cuba Libre: the Starr/Garces machine, fine, expensive, characterless. Forsythia or Fork instead.','Penn\'s Landing proper (the concrete plaza by the Independence Seaport Museum): a wasteland; the piers north of it are the good bit.'],
   known:['Independence Hall + Liberty Bell (free; timed tickets for the Hall)','Elfreth\'s Alley','First Friday gallery walk (Sep 4, Oct 2)','Race Street Pier & Cherry Street Pier','Christ Church, Franklin\'s grave','Museum of the American Revolution'],
   main:['History morning: Independence Hall 9am, Franklin Court, Christ Church, Elfreth\'s, then Menagerie coffee','Forsythia or Fork for dinner, Almanac or Sassafras after','Watch the sunset from Race Street Pier'] },
 { k:'soc', lbl:[39.9458,-75.1478], name:'Society Hill', short:'Society Hill', tag:'the prettiest brick', verdict:'yes', london:'Hampstead village streets, or the Georgian bits of Islington', nyc:'Brooklyn Heights',
   poly:['8/Walnut','1/Walnut',[39.9450,-75.1400],[39.9430,-75.1410],'1.5/Lombard','8/Lombard'],
   worth:'Yes: for the walk (the largest concentration of original 18th-century houses in the US), and because three of the five best restaurants in the city (Provenance, Zahav, Bloomsday) sit within a block of each other on 2nd Street. Dead quiet otherwise, which is the charm.',
   vibe:'Georgian and Federal rowhouses restored in the 1960s (a famous urban-renewal case study: it was a slum, then it was the richest zip code in the city), brick pavements, gas lamps, hidden walkways (Philip St, American St, Bingham Court), Delancey and Spruce and Pine between 2nd and 8th, three I.M. Pei towers by the river, and Headhouse Square at the bottom with the 1745 Shambles and the Sunday farmers market. Old money, quiet, almost no shops. Nothing to do here except walk and eat, and both are as good as the city gets.',
   avoid:['Nothing really; it is Society Hill. Just don\'t expect a bar scene: Bloomsday and the Zahav bar are it.'],
   known:['Headhouse Square and its Sunday farmers market','the walking mile of Delancey, Spruce and Pine between 2nd and 8th','Physick House, Powel House','Zahav, Provenance, Bloomsday'],
   main:['Bloomsday for a glass of wine at 5pm, then Provenance if you got the booking','Wander Delancey/Philip/American Streets after dark, gas lamps on'] },
 { k:'qv', lbl:[39.9395,-75.1462], name:'Queen Village', short:'Queen Village', tag:'low-key, local, excellent', verdict:'yes', london:'the residential streets between Columbia Road and Broadway Market', nyc:'Carroll Gardens',
   poly:['6/South','1.5/South',[39.9430,-75.1410],[39.9375,-75.1440],'6/Washington'],
   worth:'Yes, and it is the one you would probably choose to live in if you came back. Small rowhouses, corner bars, Fabric Row, and a startling density of excellent small restaurants (Royal Sushi, Ambra, Little Fish, Cry Baby, Fitz on 4th, Rougarou) with no tourists at all.',
   vibe:'South of South Street, east of 6th, down to Washington Avenue. Once Swedish, then Jewish (Fabric Row on 4th Street is the remnant: century-old textile shops mixed with vintage, Bus Stop Boutique, Brickbat Books), now young families and cooks. Two- and three-storey brick, tiny front gardens, corner bars that have been there forever (New Wave, For Pete\'s Sake, Royal Tavern), Philly AIDS Thrift on 5th (a cult), Head House Square at the top. It feels like a proper neighbourhood rather than a "district", and its restaurants are the kind that would be a big deal in London and here are just where locals eat on a Tuesday. Nothing to "visit"; everything to come back to.',
   avoid:['South Street itself, its northern boundary: see the separate entry. Queen Village starts one block south of it.','Nothing else. It is hard to have a bad time here.'],
   known:['Fabric Row (S 4th St) and its shops','Royal Sushi & Izakaya (hardest booking in the city)','Philly AIDS Thrift (cult), Brickbat Books','corner bars: New Wave, For Pete\'s Sake'],
   main:['A whole evening on 3rd/4th between South and Christian: Rougarou pastries, Cry Baby, Ambra, then New Wave Cafe','Sunday: Headhouse market, then Fabric Row'] },
 { k:'sst', lbl:[39.9440,-75.1545], name:'South Street (Broad to Front)', short:'South St', tag:'the tacky strip', verdict:'through', london:'Camden High Street', nyc:'St Mark\'s Place in its bad years',
   poly:['14/Lombard','1.5/Lombard','1.5/Bainbridge','14/Bainbridge'],
   worth:'Walk along it once because you will anyway (it is between you and Queen Village / the Italian Market), and for four specific things: Magic Gardens, Tattooed Mom, Bob & Barbara\'s, Brickbat/Repo Records. As a destination: no.',
   vibe:'The 1970s-80s counterculture strip that "South Street" the song is about, now mostly vape shops, cheesesteak tourist traps (Jim\'s, with a queue, and it is fine), sneaker stores, piercing parlours, and bars that get rowdy on Saturday nights (there was a mass shooting here in 2022; police presence is heavy at weekends now). Between about 2nd and 6th it still has some of its old scruffy charm and a few genuine survivors (Tattooed Mom, Repo Records, Philly AIDS Thrift just off it, Isaiah Zagar\'s mosaics on every other wall). West of Broad it becomes "South Street West", quieter and better (Rex at the Royal, Bob & Barbara\'s, Ants Pants). Not dangerous by day; loud, drunk and a bit charmless late on weekends.',
   avoid:['Jim\'s Steaks queue (Angelo\'s is 5 minutes away and better).','South Street between 2nd and 8th after 11pm on Fridays/Saturdays.','The Theatre of Living Arts as a venue is fine; the bars around it are not.'],
   known:['Philadelphia\'s Magic Gardens','Tattooed Mom','Bob & Barbara\'s (west of Broad)','Repo Records, Brickbat Books (just off)'],
   main:['Magic Gardens late afternoon, then straight down 4th Street into Queen Village'] },
 { k:'bv', lbl:[39.9376,-75.1620], name:'Bella Vista & the Italian Market', short:'Bella Vista', tag:'9th Street, all of it', verdict:'yes', london:'Exmouth Market / Clerkenwell crossed with Brixton Market', nyc:'Arthur Avenue in the Bronx, but walkable from your flat',
   poly:['11/South','6/South','6/Washington','11/Washington'],
   worth:'Yes, often: it is 15 minutes from your flat and holds Angelo\'s, Fiorella, Mawn, Blue Corn, Grace & Proper, Le Caveau and the market itself. Go on a Saturday morning once for the full thing, then on weeknights for the restaurants.',
   vibe:'The 9th Street Italian Market (oldest continuously operating outdoor market in the US, since the 1880s) runs from Christian down to Wharton: produce stalls under corrugated awnings, barrel fires in winter, Di Bruno Bros\' original cheese shop, Fante\'s kitchenware, Isgro cannoli, Sarcone\'s bread, Claudio\'s, Anthony\'s espresso, and, since the 1990s, an equal number of Mexican taquerias, bakeries and grocers (Blue Corn, South Philly Barbacoa) that are now the best food on the street. Rocky ran through it. The blocks either side (Bella Vista proper: 6th to 11th, South to Washington) are quiet rowhouse streets with a Catholic church every four blocks, and hold three of the best small places in the city (Fiorella, Mawn, Grace & Proper). Honest caveats: the market stalls are hit and miss (some produce is supermarket-grade), the famous red-gravy restaurants (Ralph\'s, Dante & Luigi\'s, Villa di Roma) are nostalgia not cooking, and 9th Street south of Washington gets scrappier.',
   avoid:['Ralph\'s and Dante & Luigi\'s unless you want a 1950s Italian-American time capsule; pick one at most.','Pat\'s and Geno\'s at 9th & Passyunk (the southern tip): walk past for the neon, eat at Angelo\'s.','The market on a Monday (half closed).'],
   known:['9th Street market stalls (mornings, Tue-Sun)','Angelo\'s Pizzeria','Mawn, Fiorella, Blue Corn, South Philly Barbacoa','Grace & Proper, Le Caveau above the Good King Tavern','Magic Gardens on South St'],
   main:['Saturday morning: market, Isgro cannoli, Anthony\'s espresso, then Angelo\'s at 11 before the queue','Dinner at Fiorella or Mawn, wine at Le Caveau'] },
 { k:'gh', lbl:[39.9410,-75.1720], name:'Graduate Hospital (SoSo)', short:'Grad Hospital', tag:'residential, brunchy', verdict:'through', london:'Clapham (the newer, more expensive bit)', nyc:'Park Slope\'s southern blocks',
   poly:['14/South','24.5/South',[39.9385,-75.1900],'24/Washington','14/Washington'],
   worth:'Pass through it on the way to the river or Point Breeze; aim for it only for Banshee, Rex at the Royal, or Ants Pants brunch. Pleasant, safe, and a bit boring.',
   vibe:'South of South, west of Broad, named after a hospital that closed in 2007. Rowhouses bought up by young professionals in the 2010s ("SoSo" was an estate-agent name that half stuck), brunch cafes, a Sprouts supermarket, dog parks, three good bars (Grace Tavern, Sidecar, the Cambridge), Rex at the Royal in the restored 1920 Royal Theater on South Street West, and Cafe Ynez down on Washington. Nothing wrong with it. Nothing much to it either.',
   avoid:['Washington Avenue itself: a six-lane road with big-box stores; cross it, don\'t walk along it.','Expecting a "scene": there isn\'t one; the bars are locals\' bars.'],
   known:['South Street West (Rex at the Royal, Ants Pants brunch)','Sidecar Bar & Grille, Grace Tavern','Banshee (new)'],
   main:['Ants Pants for an Australian brunch, then walk to the river','Rex at the Royal for a Southern dinner in a restored theatre'] },
 { k:'pass', lbl:[39.9235,-75.1665], name:'East Passyunk & South Philly', short:'East Passyunk', tag:'the real food neighbourhood', verdict:'yes', london:'Bermondsey Street / Peckham Rye, with old Italian nonnas', nyc:'Carroll Gardens / Cobble Hill, or Astoria for the food density',
   poly:['14/Washington','6/Washington','6/Wolf','14/Wolf'],
   worth:'Yes, absolutely, and more than once. It is the most concentrated stretch of chef-owned restaurants in the city (River Twice, Ember & Ash, Sao, El Chingon, Le Virtù, Townsend, Noord, Fountain Porter, Bok Bar), in a proper old rowhouse neighbourhood, 30 minutes\' walk or 10 on the Broad Street Line. Locals rate it above Fishtown for eating; they are right.',
   vibe:'East Passyunk Avenue runs diagonally against the grid from Pat\'s & Geno\'s at 9th & Wharton south-west to Broad & McKean, past the "singing fountain" at Tasker: independent shops (Occasionette, Nice Things Handmade), old-school Italian (Marra\'s closed in 2025, Le Virtù carries on), coffee (Black N Brew, Supérette), a great dive (Ray\'s Happy Birthday Bar), and restaurant after restaurant. Around it, the deep South Philly grid: identical two-storey brick rows, awnings, plastic-covered sofas, Italian flags next to Vietnamese and Mexican and Cambodian ones (South Philly is now the most immigrant-dense part of the city; the food follows), Bok (a huge 1936 vocational school turned into studios, with a rooftop bar and Irwin\'s restaurant on top), Termini cannoli, John\'s Roast Pork by the railway. Real, unpretentious, occasionally rough at the edges (some blocks south of Snyder, east of 6th, are poorer and emptier). Bomb Bomb Bar and Heavy Metal Sausage are down here in the deep south; take a cab back late.',
   avoid:['Pat\'s and Geno\'s (once past for the neon, never to eat).','Walking back to Center City alone after midnight through the western blocks (Broad & Snyder area): take the subway or a cab, not because it is dangerous, but because it is dead and dark.','Passyunk Ave on a Saturday night without a booking: everything is full.'],
   known:['East Passyunk Ave (Wharton to Broad)','Bok Bar rooftop (season to early Oct) and Irwin\'s','River Twice, Ember & Ash, Sao, El Chingon, Fountain Porter','Termini Bros, Isgro','Bomb Bomb Bar and the deep South Philly Italian seafood joints'],
   main:['Bok Bar at sunset (view of the whole city), then dinner on Passyunk','A whole Saturday: Italian Market → Passyunk Ave → Ray\'s Happy Birthday Bar','River Twice for the tasting-menu night'] },
 { k:'pb', lbl:[39.9300,-75.1780], name:'Point Breeze & Newbold', short:'Point Breeze', tag:'off the map, on the rise', verdict:'through', london:'Peckham ten years ago, but with worse transport', nyc:'Bed-Stuy circa 2012',
   poly:['14/Washington','24/Washington','24/Snyder','14/Snyder'],
   worth:'Not a destination. Two or three specific places (Hardena, American Sardine Bar, Ultimo) justify a detour; the neighbourhood itself is patchy: renovated blocks next to vacant ones, and it thins out fast west of 20th.',
   vibe:'West of Broad, south of Washington: historically Black South Philly, gentrifying unevenly since 2010 (Newbold is the estate-agent name for the north-east corner around Passyunk & Broad). New-build "modern" boxes next to boarded rowhouses, a few excellent small businesses (Hardena\'s Indonesian, American Sardine Bar, Ultimo coffee, South Philly Tap Room, Second District brewery, the Melrose Diner), long empty stretches, and Point Breeze Avenue itself, which is a struggling commercial strip. Fine by day; not somewhere to wander aimlessly at night, more from emptiness than danger.',
   avoid:['Wandering west of 20th Street after dark.','Point Breeze Ave as a "high street".'],
   known:['American Sardine Bar','Hardena Indonesian','Ultimo Coffee','Melrose Diner (mid-century Philly)'],
   main:['Hardena lunch, Ultimo after','American Sardine Bar as the after-Passyunk drink'] },
 { k:'pen', lbl:[39.9300,-75.1480], name:'Pennsport & the Delaware Ave strip', short:'Pennsport', tag:'mummers, big-box, the river', verdict:'skip', london:'the Old Kent Road', nyc:'Red Hook without the charm',
   poly:['6/Washington','0.5/Washington','0.5/Snyder','6/Snyder'],
   worth:'Skip, unless you are going to Herman\'s Coffee or Musi (a tiny, excellent BYOB on Morris St, the one reason a food person comes here) or the Mummers Museum out of anthropological interest.',
   vibe:'The riverside strip south of Queen Village: Irish-Catholic South Philly, home of the Mummers (the New Year\'s Day parade clubs have their halls on 2nd Street, "Two Street"), rowhouses that have not gentrified much, and along Columbus Boulevard/Delaware Avenue a wasteland of big-box retail (Walmart, Home Depot, IKEA further south), car dealerships and a casino. The waterfront trail is fine for a bike ride. Otherwise no.',
   avoid:['Columbus Blvd on foot: it is a highway with car parks.','Two Street bars on a night when the Mummers clubs are drinking, unless you want to see it (you might).'],
   known:['Musi BYOB (Morris St)','Herman\'s Coffee','the Mummers Museum','the Delaware River Trail'],
   main:['Musi if you can book it; otherwise nothing'] },
 { k:'logan', lbl:[39.9585,-75.1770], name:'Logan Square & the Parkway', short:'Parkway', tag:'museum mile', verdict:'once', london:'South Kensington\'s museum stretch, or the Mall', nyc:'Museum Mile without the park',
   poly:['15/JFK','24/JFK','24.5/SpringGarden','15/SpringGarden'],
   worth:'For the Barnes, yes, absolutely, and the Rodin garden and the Free Library. As a neighbourhood, no: it is a ceremonial boulevard, glorious on a Sunday morning, empty and windswept at night, with almost nowhere to eat or drink that isn\'t inside a hotel or a museum.',
   vibe:'The Benjamin Franklin Parkway (1917-26, modelled on the Champs-Élysées by French planners) cuts diagonally from City Hall to the Art Museum, flanked by the Free Library, the Franklin Institute, the Academy of Natural Sciences, the Cathedral, the Barnes (moved here from the suburbs in 2012, controversially, into a very good Tod Williams Billie Tsien building) and the Rodin. Around it: the Comcast towers (the tallest buildings in the city, JG SkyHigh bar on the 59th floor of the newer one), law firms, hotels, apartment towers, Sister Cities Park by Logan Circle\'s Swann fountain. Grand, and slightly lifeless. It is the part of Philly that was designed to impress rather than to be lived in, and it shows. Beautiful for a walk, especially at dusk when the museum steps light up.',
   avoid:['The Franklin Institute unless you are with a child.','Eating around here: it is hotel restaurants and salad chains. Vernick Fish is the exception and it is expensive.','Walking the Parkway late at night alone; not dangerous, just empty and long.'],
   known:['The Barnes Foundation','Rodin Museum (free garden)','Sister Cities Park and Logan Circle fountain','Comcast Center towers and JG SkyHigh bar (59th floor)','Free Library rooftop terrace'],
   main:['Barnes on a First Friday evening (music, bar, quieter galleries)','Walk the whole Parkway from City Hall to the Rocky steps at dusk','Drinks at JG SkyHigh once, for the view'] },
 { k:'fair', lbl:[39.9700,-75.1740], name:'Fairmount & the Art Museum', short:'Fairmount', tag:'villagey, near the river', verdict:'once', london:'Highbury / the streets around Highbury Fields', nyc:'Cobble Hill with a river',
   poly:['17/SpringGarden','24.5/SpringGarden',[39.9660,-75.1830],[39.9700,-75.1855],[39.9735,-75.1800],'17/Girard'],
   worth:'Once for the museum, Eastern State and Boathouse Row; then as the starting line for every river run. Fairmount Avenue itself is a pleasant village high street (Bar Hygge, London Grill, Jack\'s Firehouse, Zorba\'s, Rybrew) that you would not cross town for.',
   vibe:'North of the Parkway: the Philadelphia Museum of Art on its hill (the Rocky steps, and behind them one of the great American museums), Boathouse Row lit up on the river, Kelly Drive and the whole of Fairmount Park (the largest urban park system in the US) beginning here, and Eastern State Penitentiary\'s castle walls looming over a genuinely nice, low-rise, tree-lined residential neighbourhood of three-storey brick with a village strip along Fairmount Avenue. Young families, runners, rowers, nurses from the hospitals. Very liveable, slightly dull, better than Grad Hospital because the park is right there. Halloween Nights at Eastern State (from mid-Sept) fills it with crowds on autumn weekends.',
   avoid:['The Rocky statue queue (it is a film prop from 1982; the view from the top of the steps is the point).','Halloween Nights at Eastern State if you wanted the actual museum: they are different tickets and the day version is far better.','Fairmount Ave restaurants as a "food destination": they are neighbourhood places.'],
   known:['Philadelphia Museum of Art and the Rocky steps','Eastern State Penitentiary','Boathouse Row and Kelly Drive (the classic 8.4-mile river loop)','Lemon Hill, Fairmount Park','Fairmount Ave: Bar Hygge, London Grill, Jack\'s Firehouse'],
   main:['PMA pay-what-you-wish on Friday evenings (music in the Great Stair Hall) and first Sundays','Run or cycle the Kelly Drive loop early on a Saturday','Eastern State by day, then Bar Hygge'] },
 { k:'cal', lbl:[39.9606,-75.1600], name:'Callowhill & Spring Garden', short:'Callowhill', tag:'lofts, venues, the Rail Park', verdict:'through', london:'the railway arches of Bethnal Green / Cambridge Heath', nyc:'the West 30s under the High Line before it was finished',
   poly:['14/Vine','7/Vine','7/SpringGarden','14/SpringGarden'],
   worth:'For a Union Transfer show, yes: it is the best mid-size venue in the city and 12 minutes from you. For the Rail Park at sunset, once. Otherwise pass through; it is half-empty by day and there is nowhere to linger.',
   vibe:'The old industrial belt between Chinatown and Spring Garden Street, cut through by the abandoned Reading Viaduct (a quarter-mile of it is now the Rail Park; the rest is graffiti and weeds). Loft conversions, parking lots, the Roman Catholic High School, Union Transfer in an 1889 baggage depot, Franklin Music Hall, Poison Heart, the Trestle Inn (a 1960s go-go whiskey bar, genuinely), Love City brewery, Cafe Lift brunch, and Doom Bar on the NoLibs edge. David Lynch lived here as an art student and called it "the Eraserhood"; that tells you the atmosphere. Nothing residential in the ordinary sense; a place you go to for one thing at night.',
   avoid:['Walking through it at 1am after a show unless you are with people: empty streets, long blocks, badly lit. Cabs are easy outside Union Transfer.','Expecting a neighbourhood: it is warehouses.'],
   known:['Union Transfer, Franklin Music Hall','The Rail Park (Broad & Noble)','Doom Bar, Poison Heart, Trestle Inn','Prohibition Taproom, Love City Brewing'],
   main:['See who is playing Union Transfer the week you arrive; go regardless of the band','Rail Park at sunset, then Doom Bar'] },
 { k:'nb', lbl:[39.9740,-75.1595], name:'North Broad & Temple', short:'N Broad / Temple', tag:'the long empty avenue', verdict:'skip', london:'Old Kent Road meets Elephant & Castle', nyc:'the Grand Concourse in the Bronx',
   poly:['12/Girard','16/Girard','16/CecilB','12/CecilB'],
   worth:'Skip, unless you have a specific reason: a show at The Met Philly (Broad & Poplar, a restored 1908 opera house, worth it if the act is right), Osteria (Vetri\'s casual place at 640 N Broad, good), South (jazz + Southern food, 600 N Broad), or the Divine Lorraine hotel for the architecture. Nothing to wander for.',
   vibe:'Broad Street north of Spring Garden: wide, fast, once grand (the Divine Lorraine, the Met, the old car showrooms), then hollow: fast food, vacant lots, the Temple University campus from Cecil B. Moore up (a normal, big, urban campus surrounded by student housing and, past it, some of the poorest blocks in the city). Not somewhere to be after dark unless you are going somewhere specific. Francisville and Brewerytown to the west are slowly gentrifying but there is little for a visitor.',
   avoid:['Wandering north of Girard on foot at night.','Temple\'s campus itself unless you have a reason; it is a campus.'],
   known:['The Met Philly (concerts)','Osteria, South','the Divine Lorraine'],
   main:['A concert at The Met if the act is right; cab both ways'] },
 { k:'nl', lbl:[39.9612,-75.1440], name:'Northern Liberties', short:'NoLibs', tag:'2nd Street, condos + old bars', verdict:'through', london:'Hoxton after the developers arrived', nyc:'Williamsburg south of the bridge, 2015',
   poly:['6/SpringGarden',[39.9585,-75.1391],[39.9575,-75.1355],[39.9640,-75.1330],[39.9668,-75.1318],[39.9690,-75.1373],'6/Girard'],
   worth:'Walk through it on the way to Fishtown; stop for Standard Tap or Cafe La Maude. As a destination it lost to Fishtown a decade ago and knows it.',
   vibe:'The first Philly neighbourhood to gentrify (late 1990s to 2000s), when it was warehouses, Polish and Ukrainian churches and artists. Now: glass condo blocks along 2nd Street and the waterfront, the rebuilt Piazza (an apartment complex with a plaza, meh), Liberty Lands community park, and the surviving originals that made it: Standard Tap (the first gastropub in Philadelphia, still very good, roof deck), North Third, Ortlieb\'s (an old jazz club, now a small venue), Cafe La Maude, Honey\'s Sit \'n Eat, Silk City diner, Yards brewery down at Spring Garden. New arrivals in 2026 (Terra Grill, Mana) are decent but corporate. It is pleasant, safe, a bit generic; the energy moved four blocks north-east.',
   avoid:['The Piazza and the 2nd Street chain-bar cluster around it (Bourbon & Branch excepted).','Delaware Avenue on foot: casino, car parks, nothing.'],
   known:['2nd Street corridor','Standard Tap, North Third, Ortlieb\'s','Liberty Lands','Yards Brewing (5th & Spring Garden)','Terra Grill (Laurent Tourondel, new 2026) and Mana (Chinese BYOB)'],
   main:['Standard Tap on a Sunday afternoon','Cafe La Maude brunch (Lebanese-French, huge portions)'] },
 { k:'fish', lbl:[39.9805,-75.1290], name:'Fishtown', short:'Fishtown', tag:'the cool one, deservedly', verdict:'yes', london:'Hackney (London Fields to Dalston), with a bit of Peckham', nyc:'Williamsburg north of Metropolitan, before the towers',
   poly:[[39.9690,-75.1373],[39.9852,-75.1346],[39.9857,-75.1276],[39.9840,-75.1190],[39.9760,-75.1225],[39.9683,-75.1280],[39.9668,-75.1318]],
   worth:'Yes, and this is where you will spend most evenings that are not in Rittenhouse or Wash West. It earns the hype: the highest concentration of the city\'s best-known restaurants (Kalaya, Suraya, Laser Wolf, Beddia, Wm Mulherin\'s, Middle Child Clubhouse, Emmett, Bastia), natural wine, three music venues, coffee roasters, bagel wars, vintage. 15 min door to door on the El from 13th St to Girard. The honest downside: it is now expensive, weekend-crowded, and the Frankford Ave strip can feel like a theme park of itself on a Saturday night; go on weeknights.',
   vibe:'A working-class Irish and Polish neighbourhood of small rowhouses along the river (the fisheries were the shad on the Delaware) that turned into Philly\'s creative district from about 2008 (Johnny Brenda\'s and Kensington Quarters were the flags) and is now fully arrived: Frankford Avenue and Front Street under the El are wall-to-wall restaurants, bars, coffee (La Colombe\'s flagship with a rum distillery), bakeries (Lost Bread, Kismet, Philly Style), record and art-book shops (Ulises, Harriett\'s), boutique hotels (Wm Mulherin\'s rooms, Anna & Bel). Between the strips: the old grid of two-storey brick, Polish churches, corner bars that haven\'t changed, Penn Treaty Park on the river with the skyline across the water. It is the neighbourhood every guide sends you to and, unusually, they are right; the criticism is that it can be a bit pleased with itself, like Hackney in 2016.',
   avoid:['Frankford Hall (Starr\'s beer garden) and the big brunch queues on Saturdays: fine, generic.','Trying to walk here from Center City at night along Delaware Ave: take the El or a cab; the walk via NoLibs is fine by day.','Booking nothing on a Friday/Saturday: you will end up at Frankford Hall.'],
   known:['Frankford Ave from Girard to Palmer','Kalaya, Suraya, Laser Wolf, Beddia, Wm Mulherin\'s (all famous, all good)','Johnny Brenda\'s (bar + upstairs venue), Kung Fu Necktie, The Fillmore','La Colombe flagship (rum distillery inside), Lost Bread, Kismet Bagels','Penn Treaty Park on the river'],
   main:['Take the El to Girard, walk north up Frankford, eat at whichever of the four you booked, drink at Next of Kin or R&D','Sunday: Kismet or Philly Style bagels, walk to Penn Treaty Park, La Colombe','Continue north into Kensington for Martha and Emilia'] },
 { k:'kens', lbl:[39.9800,-75.1405], name:'Kensington & Olde Kensington', short:'Kensington', tag:'beyond Fishtown, better value', verdict:'once', london:'Clapton / Homerton, with a hard edge further north', nyc:'Bushwick',
   poly:[[39.9690,-75.1373],'5/Girard','5/Berks',[39.9892,-75.1338],[39.9895,-75.1256],[39.9857,-75.1276],[39.9852,-75.1346]],
   worth:'For Martha and Emilia specifically, yes, and Sor Ynez\'s garden on a warm night. The neighbourhood is warehouses, breweries, artists, and cheaper rents than Fishtown; the "cool" is real but thin on the ground. Know where you are: this is also the neighbourhood whose north-eastern end (Kensington Ave around Allegheny, 2 km beyond anything on this map) is the most visible open-air opioid market in the US. The places listed here are nowhere near it, but do not wander north-east on foot.',
   vibe:'Two areas that share a name. Olde Kensington / South Kensington (west of Front, between Girard and Berks): warehouses turned into breweries (Human Robot, Evil Genius), studios, Sor Ynez\'s garden, Four Humours, ReAnimator coffee, new-build lofts. East Kensington (Frankford Ave north of Norris to Lehigh): Martha, Emilia (Vernick\'s 2026 opening, at York), Fleur\'s, Loco Pez, Pizza Brain, Philadelphia Brewing Co, some vintage. Between them, blocks that range from renovated to derelict within a hundred metres. Cheaper, rawer, more genuinely artist-y than Fishtown, and patchier. Cab back at night from Martha/Emilia (or the El from York-Dauphin, which is fine).',
   avoid:['Anything north-east of Lehigh Ave / the Kensington Ave and Allegheny corridor: not a judgement, a fact.','Walking home from Emilia or Martha late; take a cab.','Coming for the "scene": there isn\'t one yet, there are individual places.'],
   known:['Martha','Emilia (2406 Frankford)','Sor Ynez, Human Robot, Philadelphia Brewing Co','Loco Pez, Pizza Brain (pizza museum)','ReAnimator Coffee'],
   main:['Emilia is the booking of the trip if you can get it; the walk-in bar seats are the fallback','Martha on a warm evening in the garden'] },
 { k:'uc', lbl:[39.9515,-75.1900], name:'University City & Penn', short:'University City', tag:'campus + 30th St', verdict:'through', london:'Bloomsbury / UCL, plus the Imperial end of South Ken', nyc:'Morningside Heights (Columbia)',
   poly:['W31/JFK','W40/JFK','W40/Pine',[39.9482,-75.2010],'W33/Pine',[39.9500,-75.1862]],
   worth:'You will be here every weekday; the question is what is worth ten minutes either side of class. Answer: the Penn Museum (world-class, under-visited, free with a Penn ID), the ICA (free), Cira Green on a warm evening, Franklin Field for a football game, and lunch at Corio or White Dog. As a neighbourhood to spend an evening: no. Students, food trucks, chain lunch spots, and it empties out at night.',
   vibe:'Penn (Ivy, 1740, Franklin\'s university) and Drexel side by side across the river from Center City. Locust Walk is the campus spine (the Button, College Hall, the Quad), Huntsman Hall is your building at 38th & Walnut, Franklin Field (1895, the oldest college stadium and the first with two tiers) and the Palestra are at 33rd, the Penn Museum at 33rd & South, the ICA at 36th & Sansom. 30th Street Station (1933, the great hall) and the Cira/FMC towers mark the eastern edge; Cira Green is a public rooftop park 12 storeys up. Between: student housing, food trucks (some very good: the halal carts, Magic Carpet), chain lunch places, a Penn bookstore, Wawa. Drexel to the north is uglier and duller. Sabrina\'s and White Dog have been feeding professors since the 1980s. Safe, busy by day, quiet by 9pm. It is a campus.',
   avoid:['Eating on Walnut/Chestnut between 33rd and 40th except Corio, White Dog, or a truck: it is Sweetgreen and Chipotle.','Drexel\'s side of Market Street; nothing there.','40th & Market and north of it after dark; the campus edge frays fast there.'],
   known:['Huntsman Hall, Locust Walk, the Button','Penn Museum (Sphinx, Chinese rotunda)','ICA (free)','Franklin Field, Palestra','30th St Station great hall','Cira Green'],
   main:['Penn Museum on a free afternoon','Penn football at Franklin Field, Sep 26 (150th season) or Oct 10','Corio for pizza between classes, White Dog for a real lunch'] },
 { k:'wp', lbl:[39.9500,-75.2200], name:'Spruce Hill, Cedar Park & Clark Park', short:'West Philly', tag:'Victorian porches, co-ops', verdict:'once', london:'Stoke Newington / Walthamstow Village', nyc:'Ditmas Park (Victorian Flatbush)',
   poly:['W40/Market','W51/Market',[39.9455,-75.2245],[39.9440,-75.2130],[39.9482,-75.2010],'W40/Pine'],
   worth:'One or two Saturday mornings after a Wharton week: Clark Park market, Fu-Wah\'s tofu hoagie, Baltimore Avenue, the Woodlands. Genuinely lovely architecture and a food scene that is cheap, immigrant-run and mostly unwritten-about (Lao, Ethiopian, Lebanese, Vietnamese). Not a night-out neighbourhood, and it fades into rougher West Philly past about 50th Street.',
   vibe:'West of 40th: enormous Victorian twins and rows with wooden porches (built as a streetcar suburb in the 1880s), students and professors and co-op families, the Clark Park Saturday farmers market with a Charles Dickens statue (the only one in the world; he forbade them), Baltimore Avenue\'s trolley line with Dock Street brewery in an old firehouse at 50th, the Mariposa food co-op, Ethiopian restaurants (Abyssinia, with the Fiume bar upstairs), Vientiane (Lao), Manakeesh (Lebanese bakery), Fu-Wah, Green Line cafes, and the Woodlands, an 18th-century estate turned garden cemetery on the river bend where Eakins is buried and people jog. Bartram\'s Garden (the oldest botanical garden in the country) is a bike ride south. Philly\'s most West-Coast neighbourhood: earnest, leafy, a bit shabby, kind. Past 50th and north of Market it becomes poorer and you have no particular reason to go.',
   avoid:['North of Market Street west of 40th, and west of 52nd, on foot at night.','Baltimore Ave "nightlife": it is Dock Street and a couple of dives, which is fine but not a plan.'],
   known:['Clark Park (Sat farmers market, chess, Charles Dickens statue)','Baltimore Avenue','Fu-Wah banh mi, Vientiane Cafe, Manakeesh, Abyssinia + Fiume upstairs','The Woodlands cemetery','Bartram\'s Garden further south'],
   main:['Saturday: Clark Park market, Fu-Wah tofu hoagie, Woodlands, Dock Street','Bartram\'s Garden by bike or bus (free kayaking Saturdays in summer)'] },

 // ---- further out (no polygon on the map) ----
 { k:'chill', far:true, name:'Chestnut Hill, Mt Airy & the Wissahickon', short:'Chestnut Hill', tag:'stone village on the hill', verdict:'once', london:'Hampstead and the Heath, exactly', nyc:'Riverdale',
   worth:'One Saturday, for the Wissahickon gorge (the best nature in the city, by a distance) with lunch in Chestnut Hill at the top. Do it in late September or early October when the leaves turn.',
   vibe:'The north-west tip of the city, 30 min by regional rail: Chestnut Hill is a stone-built, wealthy, slightly precious village along Germantown Avenue\'s cobbles (bookshops, a good cheese shop, Morris Arboretum, Woodmere Art Museum, the Chestnut Hill Hotel). Mt Airy below it is the famous mixed, liberal, leafy neighbourhood (Weavers Way co-op, High Point Cafe). Both border Wissahickon Valley Park: 1,800 acres of gorge, forest, the car-free Forbidden Drive along the creek, Valley Green Inn, covered bridge, Devil\'s Pool. Worth the trip for the park; the villages are pleasant, not essential.',
   avoid:['Driving there on a nice Sunday: parking at Valley Green is a fight. Train + walk.'],
   known:['Forbidden Drive / Wissahickon','Germantown Avenue','Morris Arboretum','Woodmere Art Museum'], main:['Walk 8'] },
 { k:'many', far:true, name:'Manayunk & East Falls', short:'Manayunk', tag:'canal town, bro bars', verdict:'skip', london:'Richmond riverside on a bank holiday, but with sports bars', nyc:'Hoboken',
   worth:'Skip as a destination. Reach it as the end of a Kelly Drive bike ride if you like: the towpath along the canal is nice, Main Street is chain-ish bars and brunch, the "Manayunk Wall" is a famous cycling climb. East Falls next door is a quiet riverside neighbourhood with nothing for a visitor.',
   vibe:'A 19th-century mill town on the Schuylkill canal, hilly, with a Main Street that became a bar-and-brunch strip for the young-professional crowd in the 1990s and has aged into a Saturday-night stag-and-hen zone. Winnie\'s (a local institution) closed in 2025. Pretty from the towpath, ordinary up close.',
   avoid:['Main Street after 10pm at weekends.'], known:['the canal towpath','the Manayunk Wall (cycling)'], main:['End of a long Kelly Drive ride, at most'] },
 { k:'germ', far:true, name:'Germantown', short:'Germantown', tag:'history, decline, seeds of revival', verdict:'skip', london:'Deptford (history + neglect + a few good things)', nyc:'Bed-Stuy\'s outer blocks',
   worth:'Only for a specific reason: the historic houses (Cliveden, Wyck, Stenton, the Johnson House on the Underground Railroad) if colonial history is your thing, or Attic Brewing / Uncle Bobbie\'s bookshop-cafe. Otherwise no.',
   vibe:'One of the oldest settlements in Pennsylvania (1683, German Quakers and Mennonites, the first written protest against slavery in America), with the battlefield and grand colonial houses to prove it, now a struggling, largely Black, disinvested stretch of Germantown Avenue between Mt Airy and North Philly, with pockets of real energy (Uncle Bobbie\'s, Attic Brewing, some artists). Interesting to a historian; not a visitor neighbourhood.',
   avoid:['Wandering Germantown Ave or Chelten Ave at night.'], known:['Cliveden, Wyck, Johnson House','Uncle Bobbie\'s Coffee & Books'], main:['A daylight history visit if it interests you'] },
 { k:'navy', far:true, name:'The Navy Yard & the Sports Complex', short:'Navy Yard / stadiums', tag:'ships, stadiums, car parks', verdict:'once', london:'the Olympic Park at Stratford, plus Wembley on match day', nyc:'the Meadowlands, but reachable by subway',
   worth:'The stadiums, yes: Citizens Bank Park (Phillies), the Linc (Eagles), Xfinity Mobile Arena (Flyers/Sixers) all sit together at the bottom of Broad Street, 12 minutes on the subway from Walnut-Locust. Go for a game; there is nothing else there but Xfinity Live (a sports-bar mall) and parking lots. The Navy Yard next door (a decommissioned shipyard turned office campus with mothballed warships and good modern landscaping) is a curiosity for an hour on a weekend.',
   vibe:'The far south of Broad Street: FDR Park (a big Olmsted-designed park with a lake, being renovated), then the three stadiums, acres of tailgate lots, and the Navy Yard\'s mix of 1900s brick, mothballed grey warships, Urban Outfitters HQ and new-build offices. Zero neighbourhood. Total American-sport atmosphere on game days.',
   avoid:['Xfinity Live except as a place to watch an away game with a crowd.','Walking anywhere: subway to NRG station and back.'], known:['Citizens Bank Park','Lincoln Financial Field','Xfinity Mobile Arena','FDR Park','the Navy Yard'], main:['Phillies Sep 4-7 or 22-27; Flyers Sep 30; Eagles Oct 4'] },
 { k:'main', far:true, name:'The Main Line (Ardmore, Bryn Mawr, Wayne)', short:'Main Line', tag:'the old-money suburbs', verdict:'skip', london:'Surrey commuter belt: Esher, Cobham', nyc:'Westchester (Scarsdale, Bronxville)',
   worth:'Not for you, unless someone from Wharton invites you to their parents\' house, in which case go and look. It is the leafy, old-money suburban corridor along the old Pennsylvania Railroad main line: Bryn Mawr, Haverford and Villanova colleges, big houses, good schools, Ripplewood in Ardmore for a whiskey bar, Ardmore Music Hall for a gig. Suburbia, well done.',
   vibe:'The Philadelphia Story country. Pretty, quiet, expensive, entirely car-shaped, 20-40 min on regional rail from Suburban Station. The Barnes lived here until 2012 and Merion is still worth nothing now that it has moved.',
   avoid:['Spending one of your fourteen weekends here.'], known:['Bryn Mawr, Haverford, Villanova campuses','Ardmore Music Hall','Ripplewood'], main:['Only if invited'] },
];

